.TH RINI 1
.SH NAME
rini \- scrolling-tiling window system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rini
[
.B -D
] [
.B -m
.I modspec
] [
.B -c
.B never\fR|\fPalways\fR|\fPon-overflow
] [
.B -s
.I cmd
] [
.B -i
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rini
is a window system in the style of the Linux compositor niri.
Windows are columns on an endless horizontal strip;
opening a window adds a column and never resizes the others,
and the view scrolls to follow the focus.
A column can hold several windows stacked vertically.
There is a vertical list of workspaces, each with its own strip
and its own floating layer.
.PP
Like
.IR rio (1),
.I rini
serves each window its own
.BR /dev/cons ,
.BR /dev/mouse ,
.BR /dev/winname
and the rest (see
.IR rio (4)),
so ordinary programs run in it unchanged.
New windows run
.B rc -i
in a text window much like
.IR rio 's,
with these differences:
.IP \(bu
Up and Down move the cursor by lines instead of scrolling;
Shift+Up and Shift+Down scroll.
End moves the cursor to the end of the input;
Home scrolls to the top.
.IP \(bu
Return with the cursor in the output sends that line, less its prompt,
as input (C64 Return; see
.BR "set c64return" ).
Shift+Return inserts a newline without sending.
.IP \(bu
Send in the button 2 menu does nothing when nothing is selected
(rio sends the snarf buffer).
.IP \(bu
The keys can be rebound with
.BR termbind .
.PP
.I Rini
runs at the top level or nested inside
.I rio
or another
.IR rini .
.PP
The options are
.TF "-c mode"
.TP
.B -D
Debug: trace keyboard input and every 9P message to standard error.
The trace includes keystrokes, so do not type secrets while it runs.
.TP
.BI -m " modspec
The modifier that starts a chord, written
.B Mod
in bindings.
The default is
.BR Mod4
(the Super key).
Modifiers combine with
.BR + ,
as in
.B Ctrl+Alt
or
.BR Mod4+Ctrl ;
.BR Shift ,
.B AltGr
and a bare
.B Alt
are refused.
.BI leader " key
makes
.I key
(for example
.BR F12 )
a leader: press it, then the chord's key.
.TP
.BI -c " mode
When the focused column is centred:
.B never
(scroll just enough to show it),
.B always
or
.BR on-overflow .
.TP
.BI -s " cmd
Run
.I cmd
in new windows instead of
.BR "rc -i" .
.TP
.BI -i " file
Run
.I file
at startup with
.IR rc (1)
instead of opening one window.
Without
.BR -i ,
.I rini
runs
.B $home/lib/rinistart
if it exists.
.PD
.PP
The startup script has no window of its own;
its standard output and error are
.IR rini 's.
It configures
.I rini
through
.B /mnt/rini/ctl
and opens the first windows with
.BR spawn .
An unmodified
.B riostart
works too.
.SS Keyboard
Every chord is the modifier plus one key.
To see the bindings, press
.B Mod+F1
for an overlay listing them all, or
.BR "cat /mnt/rini/bindings" .
.B Mod+Shift+e
asks for a second press, then exits.
Keys that are not part of a chord, AltGr symbols included,
go to the focused window unchanged.
.SS Mouse
Button 3 on the background, a gap or a window's border opens a menu:
.BR New ,
.BR Resize ,
.BR Move ,
.BR Delete ,
.BR Hide ,
.BR Float/Tile ,
then the hidden windows (selecting one brings it back).
.B Mod
plus button 3 opens the same menu on a tiled window;
on a floating window, dragging resizes it from the nearest corner.
.B Mod
plus button 1 drags a floating window.
.B Mod
plus the wheel switches workspaces;
.B Mod+Shift
plus the wheel scrolls through the columns.
.SS "Control files"
.I Rini
posts its server as
.BI /srv/rini. user . pid
and exports the name in
.BR $wsys .
Attaching with the spec
.B ctl
gives the control tree;
.I rini
mounts it on
.B /mnt/rini
in every window's namespace and in the startup script's.
It holds
.TF bindings
.TP
.B ctl
Written, a list of commands, one per line (below).
Several lines may go in one write;
the first failing line stops the write and the error names it.
Read, the current settings:
.BR mod ,
.BR center ,
.BR c64return ,
.BR background ,
then
.B focus
(the focused window's id, 0 for none) and
.B ws
(the current workspace, from 1).
.TP
.B bindings
The key bindings, as
.B bind
commands that can be written back to
.BR ctl .
.TP
.B rules
The window rules, as
.B rule
commands.
.TP
.B state
A snapshot:
.BI "workspaces " n " current " c\fR,
then one line per window,
.BI "win " "id ws kind width label\fR,"
then
.BI "focus " id\fR.
.I Kind
is
.BR tile ,
.B float
or
.BR hidden ;
.I ws
is 0 for a hidden window;
.I width
is a tiled column's width in thousandths of the screen, else 0.
.TP
.B event
A stream of changes, one per line.
A new reader first gets the current state as events.
The events are
.BI "open " "id label" \fR,
.BI "close " id\fR,
.BI "label " "id label" \fR,
.BI "focus " id\fR,
.BI "move " "id ws" \fR,
.BI "float " id\fR,
.BI "tile " id\fR,
.BI "hide " id\fR,
.BI "unhide " id\fR,
.BI "ws " n\fR,
.BI "wsadd " n
and
.BI "wsdel " n\fR.
A reader that falls behind gets
.B overflow
and should reread
.BR state .
.PD
.PP
Labels in
.BR state ,
.B event
and
.B rules
are quoted as in
.IR rc (1)
when they contain blanks or quotes.
.SS "Control commands"
Arguments are separated by blanks;
.B rule
and
.B unrule
take
.IR rc -style
quotes,
.BR '...' ,
with
.B ''
for a quote.
.TF "termbind key action"
.TP
.BI "bind " chord " \fR[\fPonce\fR|\fPrepeat\fR]\fP " action
Bind
.I chord
to
.IR action .
A chord is
.B Mod
plus optional
.BR Ctrl ,
.B Alt
and
.B Shift
and a key, joined by
.BR + :
.BR Mod+t ,
.BR Mod+Ctrl+Left .
Keys are letters, digits,
.BR minus ,
.BR plus ,
.BR equal ,
.BR comma ,
.BR period ,
.BR space ,
.BR Return ,
.BR Tab ,
.BR Escape ,
.BR BackSpace ,
.BR Delete ,
.BR Left ,
.BR Right ,
.BR Up ,
.BR Down ,
.BR Home ,
.BR End ,
.BR Prior ,
.BR Next ,
.B Insert
and
.BR F1 \- F12 .
.B repeat
makes a held chord repeat; the default is
.BR once .
Binding a chord again replaces it.
.TP
.BI "unbind " chord
Remove a binding.
.TP
.BI "set mod " modspec
As
.BR -m .
.TP
.BI "set center " mode
As
.BR -c .
.TP
.B "set c64return on\fR|\fPoff"
With
.B on
(the default),
Return with the cursor in a text window's output
sends that line, less its prompt, as input,
as on a Commodore 64.
With
.BR off ,
it moves the cursor to the end of the input, as in
.IR rio .
.TP
.BI "set background 0x" RRGGBBAA
The background colour.
.TP
.BI "termbind " "key action"
Bind a key in text windows.
.I Key
is
.RB [ Ctrl+ ][ Shift+ ]\c
.IR key .
Actions:
.BR insert ,
.BR left ,
.BR right ,
.BR up ,
.BR down ,
.BR scroll-line-up ,
.BR scroll-line-down ,
.BR page-up ,
.BR page-down ,
.BR top ,
.BR end ,
.BR kill-line ,
.BR kill-word ,
.BR backspace ,
.BR line-start ,
.BR line-end ,
.BR output-point ,
.BR hold ,
.BR return ,
.BR newline ,
.BR interrupt ,
.BR complete ,
.BR tab .
.TP
.BI "rule '" regexp "' " props
Apply
.I props
to windows whose command, or first label, matches
.IR regexp .
A window's command is matched when it opens
.RB ( rc
for a plain window), its label when the program first sets one.
The properties are
.BR float ,
.BR tile ,
.BI "width " n
(pixels) or
.BI "width " n / d
(a fraction of the screen),
.BI "workspace " n
and, for floating windows,
.BI "rect " "x0 y0 x1 y1" \fR.
When several rules match, the later one wins for each property.
A rule with the same
.I regexp
replaces the old one.
.TP
.BI "unrule '" regexp '
Remove a rule.
.TP
.BI "spawn " \fR[\fPoptions\fR] [\fPcmd\fR]\fP
Open a window running
.I cmd
(default
.BR "rc -i" ).
The options are
.IR rio 's
.B new
options:
.BI -r " x0 y0 x1 y1" \fR,
.BI -dx " n" \fR,
.BI -dy " n" \fR,
.BR -hide ,
.BR -scroll ,
.BR -noscroll ,
.BI -cd " dir
and
.BI -pid " pid" \fR.
.TP
.B delete
Delete the focused window.
.TP
.B quit
Exit at once.
.TP
.B show-hotkey-overlay
Toggle the bindings overlay.
.TP
.I action
Any of the layout actions below, as it would appear in a binding.
.PD
.PP
The layout actions, named after niri's, are
.BR focus-column-left ,
.BR focus-column-right ,
.BR focus-column-first ,
.BR focus-column-last ,
.BR focus-window-up ,
.BR focus-window-down ,
.BR focus-window-or-workspace-up ,
.BR focus-window-or-workspace-down ,
.BR move-column-left ,
.BR move-column-right ,
.BR move-window-up ,
.BR move-window-down ,
.BR move-window-up-or-to-workspace-up ,
.BR move-window-down-or-to-workspace-down ,
.BR consume-window-into-column ,
.BR expel-window-from-column ,
.BR consume-or-expel-window-left ,
.BR consume-or-expel-window-right ,
.BR center-column ,
.BR maximize-column ,
.BR fullscreen-window ,
.BR switch-preset-column-width ,
.BR switch-preset-column-width-back ,
.BR switch-preset-window-height ,
.BR reset-window-height ,
.BR toggle-window-floating ,
.BR switch-focus-between-floating-and-tiling ,
.BR focus-workspace-up ,
.BR focus-workspace-down ,
.BR move-column-to-workspace-up ,
.BR move-column-to-workspace-down ,
.BR move-window-to-workspace-up ,
.BR move-window-to-workspace-down ;
with an argument,
.BI "set-column-width " n %
and
.BI "set-window-height " n %
(absolute, or relative with a sign, as in
.BR -10% ),
.BI "set-center " mode\fR,
.BI "focus-workspace " n\fR,
.BI "move-column-to-workspace " n
and
.BI "move-window-to-workspace " n\fR.
.PP
.I Rini
exits on
.BR quit .
It sends
.B hangup
to every window's note group and to the startup script's,
then
.B kill
a second later.
.SH EXAMPLES
A startup script,
.BR $home/lib/rinistart :
.IP
.EX
#!/bin/rc
{
	echo 'set background 0x777777FF' >/mnt/rini/ctl
	echo 'bind Mod+e once spawn acme' >/mnt/rini/ctl
	echo 'rule ''^games/'' float' >/mnt/rini/ctl
	echo spawn >/mnt/rini/ctl
} >[2]$home/lib/rinistart.log
.EE
.PP
Start
.I rini
instead of
.I rio
at boot: in
.BR $home/lib/profile ,
replace
.B "rio -i riostart"
with
.BR rini .
.PP
Watch the changes:
.IP
.EX
cat /mnt/rini/event
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF $home/lib/rinistart.log
.TP
.B $home/lib/rinistart
The default startup script.
.TP
.B /mnt/rini
The control tree.
.TP
.BI /srv/rini. user . pid
The posted server.
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B src
in the rini repository.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR rio (1),
.IR rio (4),
.IR window (1)
.SH BUGS
When
.I rini
runs nested,
.B /dev/snarf
is the parent's and
.I rini
does not serve its own.
.br
Processes that put themselves in a new note group,
.B &
jobs of the startup script among them, outlive
.IR rini .
